A differential sweep of the chunked-transfer-coding grammar (RFC 9112 7.1).

Reports 17-21 were five consecutive findings in this one area, each a narrower
slice of the same boundary, so the remaining space was swept rather than waited
for. variants.py enumerates the grammar one rule at a time -- chunk-size,
chunk-ext, chunk-data and its CRLF, last-chunk, the terminator, and the trailer
section -- with each case marked valid or malformed. back.py serves variant N at
/api/f<N>; drive.py sends every one through h1, h2 and h3 with curl (an
independent client) and flags two things:

  * a MALFORMED body delivered as a clean complete 200, and
  * any h2/h3 disagreement.

Disagreement is the signature every one of reports 17-21 had, which is why it is
the thing being looked for rather than a hand-written expected value per case.

It found five defects, including one no report had reached in twenty-one
attempts: h2 refused every chunked response whose size contained a hex letter,
because h2p_hexval clobbered AL and re-read it. Every chunk fixture in the suite
used sizes 0-9, so nothing exercised it.

It sweeps RESPONSE bodies only -- the direction all three protocols share,
since a request may be chunked over HTTP/1 alone. The request side has two
decoders of its own (chunked_decode below the in_buf bound, linnea_spill_chunked
above it), and report 22 found them disagreeing about the chunk-size line
exactly where this sweep had no reach. They are asserted against one table by
`python3 test/upload_chunked.py sizeline`, which sends every case at both sizes;
a finding here belongs in both places.

Not part of run_tests.sh: it needs its own backend on a fixed port and takes a
few minutes. The findings it produced are permanent fixtures in
test/proxy_backend.py and the matrix; this is the net, not the catch. Run it by
hand after touching any chunk decoder:

    python3 back.py 62950 &
    # point a linnea vhost's /api at 127.0.0.1:62950, then
    python3 drive.py <linnea-tls-port> test/tls/server.crt ~/curl-h3/bin/curl
